# Keystrokes of record_replay.sh's phase hand: a person at an interactive bash runs the replay check
# by hand around python3 -q and types into Python under the layers. @RECORDER@ is replaced with the
# recorder's path before the script is used.
#
# Every step waits for something the session prints, never for a time, so a slow machine only makes the
# script slower. One thing is not printed: a Ctrl+C reaches Python from the terminal, and again from
# each layer that passes the signal on (core/phobos-tools-common/phobos-signals.sh), which a layer does
# at its next look at the command, every PHB_SIGNAL_POLL_SECONDS, and a starved layer looks late. A
# pass that lands after the first would interrupt whatever is typed next, or kill a layer that has
# finished its command. So after Ctrl+C the script goes `idle`: it waits until every process of the
# job is asleep, which none is while it has a signal to pass on. What is typed after fg waits in the
# terminal's queue, where only Python reads it, since bash reads nothing while the job it resumed is
# in the foreground.
#
# Markers are printed in two halves, so the echo of the typed line can never satisfy an expectation.
expect SHELL[$]
send @RECORDER@ check --name python -- python3 -q
expect >>>
send import time
send print("OK-" + "H1")
expect OK-H1
send print("OK-" + "S1", flush=True); time.sleep(30)
expect OK-S1
key ctrl-c
expect KeyboardInterrupt
idle
send print("OK-" + "H2")
expect OK-H2
key ctrl-z
expect Stopped
send fg
send print("OK-" + "H3")
expect OK-H3
send exit(0)
expect regressions
expect SHELL[$]
send echo "STATUS-""$?"
expect STATUS-0
send exit
